 at 9:00 a.m. for a briefing on Lebanon. The briefing was at the Secret level and a transcript was taken. The substantive information has been reported in the attached memorandum C. Robin Britt (D., NC) Solomon P. Ortiz (D., TX) Ronald V. Dellums (D., CA) Beverly B. Byron (D., MD) Les Aspin, (D., WI) Norman Sisisky (D., VA) Ronald D. Coleman (D., TX) Melvin Price (D., IL), Chairman Samuel S. Stratton (D., NY) Dan Daniel (D., VA) George (Buddy) Darden (D., GA) Richard Ray (D., GA) Charles E. Bennett (D., FL) John M. Spratt, Jr. (D., SC) Frank McCloskey (D., IN) Antonio B. Won Pat (D., Guam) Floyd D. Spence, (R., SC) Robert E. Badham (R., CA) James A. Courter (R., NJ) Thomas F. Hartnett (R., SC) Marjorie S. Holt (R., MD) Ken Kramer (R., CO) David O'B Martin (R., NY) Duncan Hunter (R., CA) Elwood Hillis (R., IN) Daniel B. Crane (R., IL) Robert W. Davis (R., MI) Deputy Director Office of Near East and South Asian Analysis (NESA) Directorate of Intelligence (DDI) NESA/DDI Liaison Division Office of Legislative Liaison Approved For Release 2008/10/30: CIA-RDP90B0l370R001101530014-6 Approved For Release 2008/10/30: CIA-RDP90B01370R001101530014-6 SECRET 4. There were no follow-up requests. Liaison Division Office of Legislative Liaison Distribution: Original - OLL Record Copy 1 - DDI 1 - D/OLL 1 - OLL Chrono Copy OLL/LD~ (2 Mar 84) 25X1 25X1 Approved For Release 2008/10/30: CIA-RDP90B01370R001101530014-6","downloads":33,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp90b01370r001101530014-6","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":979021,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":2,"oai_updatedate":["2025-03-29T05:32:35Z","2026-04-03T07:27:54Z"],"publicdate":"2025-03-29T05:32:35Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp90b01370r001101530014-6: HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE BRIEFING ON LEBANON","week":1,"year":1984},{"btih":"0fe425302ffb850a6545ddab2f5781eb5739bb09","collection":["opensource_audio"],"creator":"Various","date":"2003-01-01T00:00:00Z","description":"MIDI Soundtrack for the now delisted Doujinsoft Racing Title \"Xelphi -Second Limit\"), a game similar to F-Zero. A lot of people might also remember this as \"That game Vinny Vinesauce played years ago with the really good MIDI's\". \u00a0I recorded the music using the Roland SC-55, SC-880 (Rackmount 88-Pro), SC-8850 and Yamaha MU2000) The .txt file included has a readme with more info. Also included the .mid soundtrack as well. --- Track List + Reasoning: 01 - Blue Surf & Wind (Roland SC-880): Personal preference \u00a0Original Title: \"New Song*\" | Composer: Unknown 02 - Green Night Of Calm City (Yamaha MU2000): XG MIDI file \u00a0Original Title: \"ACTIVE CITY -(Rock Fusion)-\" | Composer: Hideo Yamazaki 03 - Yellow Sand Ocean (Roland SC-880): Personal preference \u00a0Original Title: \"JDRIVE*\" | Composer: Unknown 04 - Red Lava In Twin Volcano (Yamaha MU2000): XG MIDI file \u00a0Original Title: Unknown | Composer: Unknown 05 - Black Sky Is Falling (Roland SC-8850 - SC-88Pro MAP): 88 MIDI file, but my SC-880 was having weird issues playing the file, so I use the SC-8850 instead. \u00a0Original Title: \"TMP_T2_R*\" | Composer: Unknown 06 - Toward Unlimited Horizon (Yamaha MU2000): Not a fan of the drums here but the Bass & Lead patch sounded the least goofy on the MU2000 in my opinion. \u00a0Original Title: Poured | Composer: Yuzuru Honda 07 - Dive To Sunset (Roland SC-880): Personal preference \u00a0Original Title: \u6d77\u8cca\u306e\u5947\u8972 / Pirate Surprise | Composer: KeNji 08 - Escape From Jail (SC-880): 88 MIDI file \u00a0Original Title: Unknown | Composer: Yuzuru Honda 09 - Everlasting Cold (SC-880): 88 MIDI file \u00a0Original Title: Unknown | Composer: Yuzuru Honda 10 - Far From The Globe (SC-880): Personal preference \u00a0Original Title: \u6700\u5f8c\u306e\u6c7a\u6226 / Final Decisive Battle | Composer: KeNji 11 - Secret 1 (Roland SC-55): I felt the SC-55's distortion guitar sounded the least bad in this song. \u00a0Original Title: Unknown | Composer: Unknown 12 - Secret 2 (Roland SC-880): 88 MIDI file (In fact the title of the MIDI is literally \"ShockWave\", likely as the, well, \"Shockwave\" patch is used in the song.) \u00a0Original Title: ShockWave | Composer: K.Tateoka, Yuzuru Honda 13 - Secret 3 (Roland SC-880): Sounded the best out of all the modules I tried. \u00a0Original Title: FRUITS EDIT2* | Composer: Unknown 14 - Secret 4 (Roland SC-880): Personal preference \u00a0Original Title: MEIKYUU2002* | Composer: Unknown 15 - Secret 5 (Roland SC-880): 88 MIDI file \u00a0Original Title: TMP_T2_R* | Composer: Unknown 16 - Ending (Yamaha MU2000): I thought the MU2000's Brightness patch sounded best for this song compared to the ones in the various Sound Canvas modules. \u00a0Original Title: \u9418\u306e\u97f3 / Bell Sound | Composer: KeNji 17 - Ending 2 (Roland SC-55): I thought the SC-55's drums were the best fit for this song. \u00a0Original Title: \u96ea\u3069\u3051 / Snow | Composer: KeNji \u00a0 18 - Result (Roland SC-880): Personal preference \u00a0Original Title: Atmosphere | Composer: KeNji 19 - Title (SC-880): Personal preference, though I felt the song sounded good no matter which module was used. \u00a0Original Title: GR6* | Composer: Unknown ---","downloads":296,"format":["24bit Flac","Archive BitTorrent","Item Tile","MIDI","Metadata","PNG","Spectrogram","Text","VBR MP3"],"identifier":"xelphi-second-limit-dtm-selection-ost","indexflag":["index","nonoindex","uncurated"],"item_size":755777143,"mediatype":"audio","month":3,"oai_updatedate":["2024-04-11T23:34:08Z","2024-04-12T14:52:49Z"],"publicdate":"2024-04-11T23:34:08Z","subject":["xelphi","xelphi second limit","senusenu","midi",".mid","dtm"],"title":"Xelphi -Second Limit-: DTM Selection OST","week":3,"year":2003},{"backup_location":"ia906801_25","collection":["internetarchivebooks","inlibrary","printdisabled","fav-dnedjine12","fav-irisrose25"],"contributor":"Internet Archive","creator":"Hoffman, Sharon","date":"2004-01-01T00:00:00Z","description":["285 pages ; 22 cm","Includes bibliographical references (pages 265-267)","Secret #1-power -- Secret #2-purpose -- Secret #3-precious acceptance -- Secret #4-prayer -- Secret #5-praise -- Secret #6-patience -- Secret #7-perseverance -- Secret #8-perspective -- Secret #9-privilege -- Secret #10-past your past -- Secret #11-purity -- Secrets #12-passion"],"downloads":30,"external-identifier":["urn:acs6:giftedwoman12sec0000hoff:epub:aa4737d3-07d8-4bb4-8079-c66eebf29191","urn:acs6:giftedwoman12sec0000hoff:pdf:cd09c028-ce40-4e3a-b54b-e9364e315451","urn:lcp:giftedwoman12sec0000hoff:lcpdf:a53afbeb-7192-4a08-b178-44d76e24786d","urn:lcp:giftedwoman12sec0000hoff:epub:dd63cca5-b847-4dca-b65c-f4afa282dd08"],"foldoutcount":0,"format":["ACS Encrypted EPUB","ACS Encrypted PDF","Abbyy GZ","Cloth Cover Detection Log","Contents","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Dublin Core","EPUB","Item Tile","JSON","LCP Encrypted EPUB","LCP Encrypted PDF","Log","MARC","MARC Binary","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Original JP2 Tar","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","Text PDF","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"giftedwoman12sec0000hoff","imagecount":294,"indexflag":["index","nonoindex","scanned_by_ia"],"item_size":940161390,"language":"eng","mediatype":"texts","month":0,"oai_updatedate":["2018-09-07T11:55:31Z","2018-09-07T11:56:14Z","2025-07-24T20:10:54Z"],"publicdate":"2018-09-07T11:56:14Z","publisher":"Colorado Springs, Colo. : Life Journey","subject":"Women -- Religious life","title":"The G.I.F.T.ed woman : [12 secrets for a life that impacts eternity]","week":0,"year":2004},{"btih":"e7c8add78e1b6cdd0091356349616c074582564e","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1993-03-08T00:00:00Z","description":"Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Scud Directorate of Intelligence Intelligence Memorandum Office of African and Latin American Analysis 8 March 1993 Liberia: What If Taylor Departs the Scene? Summary \ufffd Charles Taylor\ufffdthe single most powerful leader involved in Liberia's civil war--has survived several recent attempts on his life by disgruntled rebels and he continues to be at some risk of assassination or overthrow. In our view, Taylor's departure could dramatically change the political and military situation in Liberia. He has no obvious successor and his departure probably would lead to a major--and potentially violent--power struggle within his rebel group. In the most likely scenario, National Patriotic Front of Liberia (NPFL) rebel military forces would spat into various armed factions whose military focus would shift from attacking the West African regional force (ECOMOG) to protecting their fiefdoms. Economic and political order in Taylorland probably would break down and ethnic-based violence against anti-Taylor Liberian forces probably would increase. Although individual rebel leaders might agree to internationally-sponsored peace negotiations as their military and economic situations worsened, any diplomatic initiatives would be complicated by a burgeoning number of military leaders. In the less likely event that a new military strongman emerged, we believe that NPFL military strategy would remain coherent, but nonetheless would shift to reflect a greater emphasis on protecting rebel strongholds from attacks by anti-Taylor Liberian factions. This memorandum was prepared by Office of African and Latin American Analysis, with contributions from ALA, and Office of Leadership Analysis. Comments and queries are welcome and may be directed to Chief, Africa Division, Secret Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 \"S 1 IG (b)(3) Like Taylor, a new strongman would be unlikely to negotiate seriously unless he was convinced that no viable military options remained. In the least likely scenario--but the best case for US interests--a moderate political leader could emerge from the leadership struggle and be willing and able to seriously negotiate a peace settlement. The United States is unlikely to be able to exercise much influence over the succession struggle, but later might be able to sway individual commanders with political and economic inducements. Fragmentation of the NPFL risks a worst-case scenario of increased ethnic warfare, widespread humanitarian problems, and spillover of instability into neighboring countries. This would spur greater international _pressure for UN and US intervention and massive humanitarian relief. How Might Taylor Go? Recent assassination attempts against Taylor underline the continuing threat to his leadership, particularly from his rebel commanders. In our view, although the precautions he has taken to monitor discontent and protect himself help minimize the risk, further military setbacks and growing internal turmoil within the NPFL could lead to another attempt at any time.1 Assassination or Overthrow: The most likely scenario for Taylor's removal from the scene would be his assassination or overthrow by a disgruntled commander from the Gio or Mano ethnic group. Over the past six months, The most recent attempt reportedly occurred last December, when several Gio and Mano special forces commanders ambushed his vehicle near Kakata. Taylor, who is an Americo-Liberian, has often spurred animosity among his ethnic Gio and Mano commanders by his brutal punishment of perceived disloyalty or military failures. In one instance, Taylor executed several popular Gio and Man9 officers for allowing anti-Tav17 armed groups to take over western Liberia last August. I Fleeing the Scene: Although less likely than the previous scenario, there also is a possibility that Taylor might flee Liberia--particularly if he determined that he was in imminent danger of being ousted or assassinated or if he concluded that he had exhausted all acceptable military and diplomatic options. Unlike former Liberian President Doe, who remained in Monrovia even after it became clear that his safety was in jeopardy, we believe Taylor probably would cut his losses rather than try to retain power at any costs. Taylor has accumulated substantial financial assets through his control over much of Liberia's exportable resources; he has a residence in Burkina, where President Compaore reportedly has assured him asylum. iTaylor has recently created three new internal security services in an effort to monitor dissent and protect himself from other possible attempts against him from disgruntled rebels, His brutal suppression of other assassination attempts also probably serves as a deterrent. Secret (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 eci e (b)(3) Who are Some Potential Initial Successors?2 In our view, there is no clear successor to Taylor, and his departure by any means probably would spark an intense power struggle within the NPFL. Taylor has limited the sphere of authority of all of his lieutenants and has eliminated--or reined in--those, including civilians, whom he viewed as potential challengers. Potential Military Leaders: We believe a Gio or Mano would have the best chance of quickly seizing and consolidating power given that the NPFL is dominated by Gio and Mano troops. Based on our knowledge of past schemes to oust Taylor, we believe that Interior Minister Dokie, who is a Gio, would be best positioned to attempt a power grab from the rebel leader. Dokie probably would have an edge over other rebel commanders given his contacts in both NPFL military and political circles and reputed connections in Burkina and Libya, He probably would be able to count on Burkina and Libya to continue providing,pateriel or other support in an effort to maintain ties to the NPFL established by Taylor.\" Dokie also would likely have support from senior NPFL commanders, including Chief of Staff Musa, a Gio, and his deputy General John Teah, who is either Gio or Mano, and whose conciliatory style is widely popular within the NPFL, Dokie, however, lacks Taylor's charisma and probably the leadership skills needed to hold the fractious rebel movement together for more than several months. Dokie also lacks Taylor's sophistication and probably would have major problems maintaining Taylor's effective overseas network of business supporters and political contacts--a key element in maintaining military supply lines to the NPFL. Alternatively, Musa, who has often expressed anger over Taylor's apparent indifference to Gio and Mano ethnic interests, might make a bid to replace Taylor if he believed Taylor had become a major threat to his or Gio interests. As the latter's Chief of Staff, Musa is more' familiar with all aspects of Taylor's operation than any of his other commanders, and probably could, at least temporarily, forcibly consolidate control. He probably would have a more difficult time galvanizing political support than Dokie, however, because his reputation for ruthlessness has hurt his standing among the rank and file. Moreover, Teah, who commands deep loyalty among the soldiers, reportedly has little respect for Musa and probably would work to undermine him. Based on fragmentary reporting, we believe Musa is the only potential successor on the horizon who might avoid contacts with the United States; he has accused Washington of undermining the NPFL. Possible Political Contenders: Although less likely than a military successor, a political leader could emerge from among the many advisers in Taylor's government in Gbarnga. If Taylor fled, the advisers might have a chance to capitalize on confusion in the military and gain the upper hand. The Vice President of Taylor's National Patriotic Reconstruction Assembly (NPRAG) government, Enoch Dogolea, and the former Managing Director of Roberts Airfield, Randolph Cooper, are the two most likely civilian successors. Dogolea is a moderate Gio with broad support among his ethnic 2See Appendix: Potential Successors to Charles Taylor. both Libya and Burkina discreetly provide small amounts of military 31 assistance to the NPFL.' 3 Sccrct (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(3) Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Secret (b)(3) kinsmen and wide acceptability with tribal chieftains in his Nimba County homeland, Cooper is an Americo-Liberian who has provided financial support to the rebels and might be able to unite Americo-Liberians within the NPFL. Scott Toweh, who is popular with Nimban civilians and has some contacts abroad, might be able to gain support nationally as well as within the NPFL. None of these moderate political leaders is likely to gain the support of the NPFL's more militant military officers, however, seriously hampering their political prospects. Moreover, even if one of these leaders succeeded initially in gaining some support among NPFL military commanders, we doubt he could hold the force together for more than two or three months. As an Americo-Liberian, Cooper would have particular difficulty winning over the distrustful Gio and Mano majority in the NPFL. What are Some Potential Post-Taylor Scenarios? If the NPFL Splinters: In our view, regardless of who initially succeeded Taylor, the latter's departure most likely would set off the fragmentation of the NPFL within three months or so. Key indicators that no new leader was taking hold and building broad support among Taylor's political inner circle and rebel commanders would include: -- Signs that local rebel commanders were establishing autonomous fiefdoms throughout Taylorland in an effort to protect their ethnic and economic interests. We would expect to receive increased reporting of regional rebel leaders independently \"taxing\" local civilians and refusing to obey orders given by top commanders. Rival, and increasingly contentious, NPFL leadership claims and statements on the desirability of negotiating peace from Taylor's various former lieutenants. Outbreaks of fighting between NPFL troops associated with different commanders. The splintering of the NPFL probably would cause economic, political, and security conditions in Taylorland to worsen considerably. Without the authority imposed by the charismatic Taylor, local commanders would gradually slip from central control, and each such armed faction could develop its own agenda, leading to potential conflict among them. Moreover, without Taylor at the helm, a number of the foreign firms currently exporting timber and other raw materials from Taylorland under contract with Taylor probably would withdraw, leaving the rebels with little access to foreign currency. In sum, any semblance of economic or political structures in Taylorland almost certainly would disintegrate, increasing prospects for local anarchy. Moreover, NPFL rebels probably would shift from a common military strategy of fending off the West African security force near Monrovia toward a more fragmented approach, with each rebel leader concentrating on protecting his individual fiefdom. Fighting also would likely become more ethnic-based, as Gio and Mano rebels moved to avenge the deaths .of kinsmen at the hands of Krahn rivals, who currently predominate in the Liberian anti-Taylor forces fighting alongside the West African security force. The Secret 4 (b)(3) Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 (b)(3) commitment and ability of NPFL rebels to continue fighting would likely wane, however, without a leader capable of reliably securing military and other supplies and organizing military campaigns. This would make these rebels more vulnerable to defeat by ECOMOG should Nigeria decide to take the potentially risky step of penetrating rebel strongholds, such as Gbarnga, to root them out. The impact of NPFL fragmentation on peace talks would be uncertain. Without a focal point, the number of players from the NPFL would multiply, limiting the prospects for a coordinated peace effort. As their economic and military situations worsened, some individual commanders would show more flexibility than Taylor has in negotiating a settlement. Nonetheless, more militant rebel commanders probably would not agree to give up local autonomy to the UN or ECOMOG unless militarily forced to do so. If A New Military Strongman Took Over: In the less likely event that a succession struggle was producing a forceful leader capable of maintaining NPFL unity, we would probably see reports that regional and local rebel commanders were beginning to defer to one of their peers on military tactics and strategy. Other indicators would be such a leader's ability to maintain Taylor's external supply network and to gain agreement from fellow rebels on a coordinated approach toward negotiations with the regional security force, other armed factions, and outside mediators. Even under this scenario, we believe a shift in the military strategy of NPFL forces would be likely; the new leader probably would focus on protecting rebel territory rather than pressing for military victories against ECOMOG. Gio rebel commanders, who have long questioned Taylor's costly attempts to take Monrovia, probably would urge the new commander to consolidate troops in rebel-held territory to minimize casualties and protect rebel strongholds from other armed factions. Such a strategy probably would shift the focus of the fighting away from the outskirts of Monrovia to western Liberia, where United Liberation Movement of Liberia for Democracy (ULIMO) forces--the greatest indigenous threat to the NPFL--are concentrated. A quick succession from Taylor to a Gio or Mano military leader probably would not increase the likelihood of a negotiated peace settlement within the next year. Like Taylor, his military leaders appear bent on maintaining their military options. Dokie would seek a negotiated settlement only if the NPFL were about to break up into factions and no longer could counter ECOMOG. Musa also has indicated on numerous occasions that he would be unwilling to negotiate away his rebels' arms and freedom of movement. Should Dokie--or some other Gio or Mano military leader\ufffdeventually decide to negotiate, however, his ethnic ties to the troops would make him better able than Taylor to maintain the confidence and cooperation of the rank and file while negotiating their encampment and disarmament. If A Moderate Political Leader Emerged: Indicators that this least likely scenario was emerging might include moves by Taylor's military leaders to choose a politician to represent them in negotiations with West African leaders and other outsiders and to broker disagreements among factions within Taylor's organization. In our view, such a scenario would become more likely if Taylor's military successors determined that their military prospects were dim. Under such circumstances, the rebels probably would be looking primarily for assurances of personal safety and economic incentives\ufffdincluding Secrct 5 (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(3) Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Secrct (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(3) food, jobs, and financial support--to lay down their arms. They also would likely seek a ceasefire in place and assurances of NPFL representation in a new interim government prior to elections. Significant levels of distrust and fear of ethnic retribution would seriously complicate negotiations, however, and the rebels almost certainly would insist on maintaining some military capability until a government they had confidence in was elected. What are the Opportunities and Risks for the United States? The scenario most favorable to US interests--although also the least likely--would be for the NPFL to hold together and a moderate political leader to emerge from the leadership struggle. Washington, however, probably would be unable to exercise much influence on the succession process within the NPFL in the immediate aftermath of Taylor's departure. Although NPFL leaders maintain contact with US officials\ufffdand, with the possible exception of Musa, probably would continue to do so--they are rarely swayed by US advice, believing most US officials are biased against the NPFL. Washington would have a better chance of influencing rebel behavior with political and economic inducements at a later stage\ufffdparticularly if the NPFL fragmented and continued ECOMOG military pressure and sanctions worsened the military and economic situation for individual commanders. In the less likely event that a new strongman such as Dokie emerged, the US could encourage him to negotiate by assuring him that the United States and UN would work to protect NPFL fighters from retribution at the hands of ECOMOG and its Liberian allies. Fragmentation of the NPFL would greatly increase the potential for further humanitarian problems in Liberia and in neighboring countries. A complete breakdown in NPFL discipline, internecine stlife,'and increasingly ethnic-based fighting among warring Liberian groups would displace more citizens within the country, complicate international relief efforts there, and boost refugee flows into neighboring countries. The deteriorating security situation would cause Liberians seeking refuge to flood into the crowded capitol, exacerbating food and water shortages. Without Taylor and his inner circle as pegpoints, international relief workers probably would run into many of the problems they have encountered in Somalia--for example, dealing through, as well as fending off, local warlords bent on procuring their share of relief provisions. As refugee outflows increased and fighting by NPFL splinter groups expanded to border areas, neighboring countries such as Sierra Leone, Guinea, and Ivory Coast probably would request increased US military assistance to minimize potential spillover of the conflict. Local NPFL commanders probably would be tempted to expand their activities across borders and to sell off large weapons caches for personal gain, increasing banditry and potentially arming anti-regime elements, particularly in Sierra Leone and Guinea. Although the risk to the roughly 100 US citizens in Monrovia probably would not increase much above present levels as long as ECOMOG remained, the 14 to 30 Americans still residing in rebel-held territory would be in significantly greater danger. Without Taylor to foster some sense of discipline among the rebels, US citizens living and traveling in the countryside almost certainly would run an even greater risk of suffering the same fate as the five nuns allegedly killed by Taylor's troops in October 1992. 6 (b)(3) Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 -Secret-(b)(3) Appendix Potential Successors to Charles Taylor Military Leaders General Isaac Musa, Chief of Staff National Patriotic Front of Liberia (NPFL) Member of Charles Taylor's inner circle... ...vehemently opposed to negotiated settlement of Liberian conflict, ..distrusts Americo-Liberians, such as Taylor,F .Gio from Nimba county...age unknown. General John Teah, NPFL Deputy Chief of Staff Widely popular in NPFL; considered peacemaker, compromiser, \ufffdreportedly has history of conflict with Isaac Musa, ..combat veteran; willing to take duty at the front, ..former major in Armed Forces of Liberia.. .of Gio or Mano ethnicity...age unknown. Civilian Officials Enoch Dogolea, Vice President, National Patriotic Reconstruction Assembly Government (NPRAG) Nominal NPFL number two, but has little role in NPFL decisionmaldng; ignored by Charles Taylor in meetings with US officials...often publicly critical of US policy in Liberia; privately seeks closer ties, _ethnic Gio; has support base among citizenry, tribal chiefs in own Nimba county, ..former Baptist preacher, high school teacher...age 40. Samuel Dokie, NPRAG Minister of the Interior Close advisor to Charles Taylor since beginning of rebellion...politically astute; deliberate, serious.. .commands broad respect, loyalty in NPFL...civil servant under late President Samuel Doe; inducted into military with other civilian officials in 1981...fled after failed coup attempt in 1983; subsequently organized Liberian exiles in Guinea, ..ethnic Gio from Nimba county...age 45. Randolph Cooper, NPFL official Managed Robertsfield airport until its fall to West African Peacekeeping force in Febru ...NF'FL spokesman in Washington, 1990; ..from wealthy family that helped set up NPFL's National Bank of Liberia, _ ...has Master's Degree in banking and finance...about 40 years old. Sccrct 7 (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(3) Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Secret (b)(3) Scott Toweh, Politician Longtime presidential aspirant...minister in Doe's government during 1980s; inducted into military with other civilian officials in 1981.. .fled Liberia in mid-1990; returned to restart political career a year later.. ..has strong tribal connections among Gios, other tribal groups...no known ties to NPFL/NPRAG...age 55. Secret (b)(1) (b)(3) (b)(3) 8 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713 Liberia Unclassified \ufffd \ufffd Capitol * Taylor's Capitol Airport Taylorland Anti-Taylor Territory ECOMOG Territory GIO Homeland MANO Homeland KRAHN Homeland Approved for Release: 2026/07/02 C06859713","downloads":0,"format":["Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","Text PDF","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-06859713","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":11134971,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":0,"oai_updatedate":["2026-09-11T20:01:08Z","2026-09-12T09:19:38Z"],"publicdate":"2026-09-11T20:01:08Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room 06859713: LIBERIA: WHAT IF TAYLOR DEPARTS THE SCENE","week":0,"year":1993},{"backup_location":"ia905608_27","btih":"55157f90de75943edbeea86fae5166aaf0dcde22","collection":["chilloutzonefm","folksoundomy"],"creator":"DJ Silence","date":"2010-01-01T00:00:00Z","description":"01. Buddysense - Aerobus [5:29] 02. Roudoujou - Sp\u00a9cialit\u00a9 Maison [2:14] 03. John Kellett - Thru The Outback [5:07] 04. Ralph Myerz And The Jack Herren Band - A Special Morning [3:46] 05. DJ Okawari - Ring [1:58] 06. Koan - Ghost In The Piramids [3:57] 07. Adham Shaikh & Tim Floyd - Snakedance [8:27] 08. Fila Brazilia - A Zed & Two L's [4:53] 09. Xylon - Lounging [5:27] 10. Pitch Black - Lost In Translation (International Observer meets Horace Remix) [3:12] 11. NuSound - Budapest Blues [3:12] 12. Koan - Geisha's Secret [4:38] 13. Lemongrass - Dinner With You [3:31] 14. REG Project - Tatouage Oriental [4:07] 15. Bliss - Long Life To You My Freind [3:29] 16. Sunshine - Immaculate Ibiza [3:19] 17. Hybrid - Every Word [4:31] 18. Moshic - Far But The Same [4:19] 19. Peter Benisch - Waiting For Snow (Part 03) [3:53] 20. Saycet - Trilogie [3:28] 21. Christ. - Glenbrook [4:10] 22. Enuui - Narayana [10:32] 23. Woob - 85-Bit [14:29] 24. Les Autres Circuits - Kaleidophon [8:44]","downloads":819,"format":["Archive BitTorrent","Book Cover","Checksums","Cue Sheet","Flac","Flac FingerPrint","Item Tile","Metadata","Ogg Vorbis","PNG","VBR MP3"],"genre":"Ambient, Chillout, PsyChill","identifier":"dj_silence_-_from_heaven_09_-_secret_2010","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":1032120752,"mediatype":"audio","month":4,"oai_updatedate":["2013