ese. I will be making a series of gameplay videos with 100% everything! Enjoy! \u697d\u3057\u3093\u3067\u304f\u3060\u3055\u3044!\ud83e\udd23 ------------------------------ Check out my other playlists! \ud83d\udc47\ud83d\udc47\ud83d\udc47 HP2 custom maps: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLMBcUyobBAaKiIUK6ALqo9xRYCJIDYCwc HP1 custom maps: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLMBcUyobBAaIhEc68dibs768_3kd2lTFC Custom map tutorials: https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLMBcUyobBAaIRwWuVWHACIE4ocnIUINF7 ------------------------------ 2:50 secret 4:50 secret 7:15 secret 7:55 secret 9:44 secret 10:36 secret 13:23 secret Source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T1XlNUg19f4 Uploader: DouziSmol","downloads":68,"format":["Archive BitTorrent","Item Tile","JSON","MPEG4","Metadata","Thumbnail","Unknown"],"identifier":"youtube-T1XlNUg19f4","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":197760253,"mediatype":"movies","month":0,"oai_updatedate":["2022-07-11T19:03:33Z","2026-06-21T00:17:09Z"],"publicdate":"2022-07-11T19:03:33Z","stripped_tags":["a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=T1XlNUg19f4\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\"","a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/channel/UCCvamSMzkc44GiHzd-yA3Og\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\""],"subject":["Youtube","video","Gaming","Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets","Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets PC","Harry Potter 2 PC","Harry Potter game","Harry Potter 2 game","Hogwarts","gaming","gamer","Harry Potter","Harry Potter Japanese","\u30cf\u30ea\u30fc \u30dd\u30c3\u30bf\u30fc\u3068\u79d8\u5bc6\u306e\u90e8\u5c4b","\u30cf\u30ea\u30fc \u30dd\u30c3\u30bf\u30fc\u3068\u79d8\u5bc6\u306e\u90e8\u5c4b \u30b2\u30fc\u30e0","\u30cf\u30ea\u30fc \u30dd\u30c3\u30bf\u30fc \u30b2\u30fc\u30e0","\u30cf\u30ea\u30fc \u30dd\u30c3\u30bf\u30fc\u3068\u79d8\u5bc6\u306e\u90e8\u5c4b PC \u30b2\u30fc\u30e0","Harry Potter 2 PC Japanese","Harry Potter Japanese version","Harry Potter and the Chamber of Secrets PC Japanese","Harry Potter 2 100% gameplay","Harry Potter 2 PC 100% gameplay","potions","snape","Severus Snape"],"title":"\u30cf\u30ea\u30fc \u30dd\u30c3\u30bf\u30fc\u3068\u79d8\u5bc6\u306e\u90e8\u5c4b PC \u30b2\u30fc\u30e0 Harry Potter 2 PC Japanese #8 Skurge Challenge secrets \u30b9\u30af\u30fc\u30b8\u30fb\u30c1\u30e3\u30ec\u30f3\u30b8 \u79d8\u5bc6","week":0,"year":2022},{"btih":"5c0fc5b6decf00243dc4f8881033b3c0eccc8815","collection":["mirrortube","social-media-video","additional_collections_video","deemphasize"],"creator":"Les Revues du Monde","date":"2015-05-05T00:00:00Z","description":"Les Revues du Monde, pour tous les curieux ! Aujourd'hui nous partons \u00e0 la d\u00e9couverte de 3 lieux incroyables que le temps a fait disparaitre, dans la puissance de la Cascade aux 7 chutes, les vertiges des Jardins suspendus de Babylone, et la grandeur de la Biblioth\u00e8que d'Alexandrie. ___________________________________________________________ Un grand merci \u00e0 Le Masuyuki pour la voix OFF dans l'intro ! https://www.youtube.com/user/lemasuyuki _______________________________________________________ Comme d'habitude, n'h\u00e9site pas \u00e0 t'abonner \u00e0 la cha\u00eene pour ne rater aucune nouvelle vid\u00e9o, et me soutenir dans ce projet. Pour me soutenir et envoyer du love : https://www.tipeee.com/les-revues-du-monde Si tu souhaites rejoindre la page Facebook : https://www.facebook.com/revuesdumonde Twitter : https://twitter.com/revuesdumonde Pour toute question, demande d'informations, ou proposition pour les prochaines RDM, tu peux me contacter \u00e0 cette adresse : lesrevuesdumonde@gmail.com ____________________________________________________________________________________________________ - JEU POUR LA NEOCAST 2015, LES 16 & 17 MAI - Pour ce jeu, il te faut trouver un mot myst\u00e8re, qui sera la cl\u00e9. Pour ceci, il va falloir r\u00e9soudre quelques \u00e9nigmes, en commen\u00e7ant pas d\u00e9chiffrer ce code secret : \" Tsyv gsqqirgiv xe uyixi, xy hizvew xvsyziv pi rsq hi pe wsgmixi wigvixi wyttswii ksyzivriv pi qsrhi, hsrx pe tviwirgi iwx tevxsyx qiqi wyv piw fmppixw eqivmgemrw. Kevhi gi rsq ir xixi tsyv pe tvsglemri ixeti \" (Indice pour le code secret : 4 ) Une fois le code d\u00e9chiffr\u00e9, tu devras trouver le symbole qui correspond au nom que le code t'as donn\u00e9. A pr\u00e9sent, tu dois trouver ce symbole quelque part cach\u00e9 sur la page Facebook des Revues du Monde, pour la suite, la solution te sera point\u00e9 du doigt. Envoie ta r\u00e9ponse \u00e0 l'adresse suivante (et uniquement \u00e0 cette adresse, si la solution ou des indices sont d\u00e9voil\u00e9s ailleurs ta participation sera nulle) : lesrevuesdumondejeu@gmail.com Merci de ne pas donner la solution ou d'indices dans les commentaires de la vid\u00e9o, sur Facebook ou nul part ailleurs. Si tu as \u00e9t\u00e9 le plus rapide \u00e0 trouver la solution, je te contacterais par e-mail \u00e0 l'adresse avec laquelle tu as envoy\u00e9 ta r\u00e9ponse. Bonne chance ! EDIT : Les deux gagnants ont \u00e9t\u00e9 trouv\u00e9 ! _____________________________________________________________________________ A bient\u00f4t pour de nouvelles aventures ! Source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J11rH3cLMi8 Uploader: Les Revues du Monde","downloads":51,"format":["Archive BitTorrent","Item Tile","JSON","Metadata","Thumbnail","Unknown","Web Video Text Tracks","WebM","h.264"],"identifier":"youtube-J11rH3cLMi8","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":162686997,"mediatype":"movies","month":0,"oai_updatedate":["2022-04-24T03:49:48Z","2026-06-08T07:11:59Z"],"publicdate":"2022-04-24T03:49:48Z","stripped_tags":["a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J11rH3cLMi8\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\"","a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/channel/UCnf0fDz1vTYW-sl36wbVMbg\" rel=\"ugc nofollow\""],"subject":["Youtube","video","Entertainment","top","3 lieux","incroyable","disparu","myst\u00e8res","jardins suspendus de babylone","biblioth\u00e8que d'alexandrie","cascade des 7 chutes","les revues du monde"],"title":"Ces lieux incroyables et disparus - RDM #10","week":0,"year":2015},{"btih":"1d76f8ffac05bac0f81a3353179442b2943763a6","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1954-02-25T00:00:00Z","description":"Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-0081OA003600790004-2 25X1 CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE.AGENCY INFORMATION REPORT SECRET COUNTRY East Germany SUBJECT Radar Installation at Kleinberndten 25X1 DATE OF INFO. PLACE ACQUIRED 25X1 This Document contains information affecting the Na- tional Defense of the United States, within the mean- ing of Title 18, Sections 793 and 794, of the U.S. Code, as amended. Its transmission or revelation of its contents to or receipt by an unauthorized persop is prohibited by law. The reproduction of this form is prohibited. DATE DISTR. NO. OF PAGES REQUIREMENT REFERENCES 1. Location: a. Theprev .ously observed radar set was still in its position about 250 meters north df Kleinberndten. Just north of the radar array a.barracks had been newly erected. There were five box-shaped vehicles in open-sided hits along the track running parallel to the main road. (See sketch in Annex I.) The installation. was located in open country. The site was bound towards Kleinberndten by a barbed wire fence. The approximate distance between the barracks and the last shed was 350 meters. 2. P} scilion of Set; The radar sot coiisiated of what appeared to be two parabolic reflectors mounted in a horizontal and vertical position respectively., The reflector, placed in a horizontal position, was supported above the ground by a solid object approximately two meters in height. The vertically mounted reflector seemed to rest with its lowest extremity in the center of the horizontal. bowl. The reflectors were of open or girder construction, approximately four to five meters in diameter. Inside the reflectors was what appeared to be a grid-like system with 'knobs' or insulators at the intersecting points. Multi-wire lines or cables fanned out from the top of the vertical reflector to the base of the set. 3. DD ee, tien of Aerials (See sketch in Annex II): Each of the four aerials consisted of a square wooden or metal, frame, support- ed above the ground by one mast approximately four meters in height. The frame was mounted in an upright position, resting with one corner on top of this mast. Two cross bare were fixed horizontally to the upper half of the mast and were connected by what appeared to be a single wire to the outer corners of the frame. The aerials had not been in position on 20 September 1953. 25X1 25X1 25 February 1954 25X1 25X1 (Not.: Washington D1.tribut1aAp0Ib6Pv dyPQr;R ease'iNG6M11$1.): CIA-RDP80-0081 OA003600790004-2 Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-0081OA003600790004-2 SECRET) 4. Yalticlea: The four aerials were erected in the spaces between the five sheds situated to the east of the set. The sheds were open on all four sides, and each contained one truck of the 'box' type. The aerials were not visibly connected to the vehicles. 5. Activity: a. At the time of observation the radar sot remained stationary - the reflectors did not rotate or tilt up or down. 25X1 25X1 b. Two men were seen to be walking between the set and the adjoining sheds. c. Two two-man Soviet patrols were observed patrolling the outer perimeter of the barbed wire fence. Five enlisted men, wearing padded winter clothing, were seen walking in Kleinberndten. There were no Russian troops billeted in the village. No other military equipment was observed in the vicinity. 6. C muni r ti pn__s: An overhead telephone line approached the rear of the site from a southerly direction and continued in a north-easterly direction towards Wollersleben. The line consisted of two pairs of bare copper wires, each wire carried-by a separate insulator fixed directly to the poles. The average height of the poles was about four meters. 25X1 SECRET) 25X1 Approved For Release - - 790004-2 Approved For Release 21f314RDP 0-00810A003600790004-2 25X1 25X1 Appro d For elease 00 111122: QfA-RDP80-00810A003600790004-2 KLF 1 N t~uy-VE N Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-00810A003600790004-2 h e, -)J~ jc me _rs la n~ i rv. ai' p ) Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-0081 OA003600790004-2","downloads":38,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp80-00810a003600790004-2","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":2752712,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":1,"oai_updatedate":["2024-06-04T13:54:28Z","2026-03-24T02:44:35Z"],"publicdate":"2024-06-04T13:54:28Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp80-00810a003600790004-2: RADAR INSTALLATION AT KLEINBERNDTEN","week":1,"year":1954},{"btih":"08d5b729a924f3dc3b345a6b2bc26554977dca6c","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1954-06-11T00:00:00Z","description":"Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-0081OA004200010001-3 634001 CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE AGENCY INFORMATION REPORT 25X1 SECRET COUNTRY SUBJECT 25X1 This Document contains information affecting the Na- tional Defense of the United States, within the mean- ing of Title 18, Sections 793 and 794, of the U.S. Code, as amended. Its transmission or revelation of its contents to or receipt by an unauthorized person is prohibited by law. The reproduction of this form is prohibited. REPORT NO. DATE DISTR. NO. OF PAGES REQUIREMENT NO. REFERENCES 11 ,Tune 1954 1. The OKSYWI3 carried the following cargoes from Poland on her voyage in the first half of February: 25X1 a. 70 tons mixed cargo including textiles, casein and lemon juice. 42 tons coal; 1 ton textiles 77 tons textiles; 17 Soviet Moskvich cars 2. On her return journey the M.S. OKSYWIE carried the following: 25X1 a. 25X1 . b. 25X1 c. 25X1 d. 25X1 e. 30 tons penicillin (sic); 55 tons grass seed; 25 tons powdered milk; 8 tons cleaning rage for machinery. 3. The OKSYWIE has been equipped with - radio apparatus 25X1 Unlike many other Polish merchant marine ships she also has a radio transmitter. SECRETA 25 tons wool; 150 tons paper 102 tons ferrochrome, in sacks; 260 tons ferrochrome, in barrels. 12 tons ball bearings. 40 tons rage; 7 (tons ?) thin pipes 25X1 25X1 25X1 25X1 25X1 ;Note: Washington Distribution Indicated By \"X\", Field Distribution By \"#\".) / Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-0081OA004200010001-3 Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-00810A004200010001-3 'SECRET 4. For the above voyage the OKSYWIE recieved new first and second officers and several new crew members: a. First Officer Stanislaw Petruszewicz. (Biadal, whom he replaced, was a U.B. agent). b. Second Officer Stanislaw Maj. c. Boleslaw Kowal, able seaman. d. Stefan Selezniow, crew member. e. Stanislaw Potoczek, appointed from a course in Gdynia, crew member. f. Alojzy Piszczek, \"hotel staff\". The last three named were former enlisted men in the navy.1 SECRET/I 25X1 25X1 25X1 Approved For Release 2005/11/22 : CIA-RDP80-00810A004200010001-3","downloads":54,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp80-00810a004200010001-3","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":1070219,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":8,"oai_updatedate":["2024-06-04T13:48:46Z","2026-03-24T04:23:38Z"],"publicdate":"2024-06-04T13:48:46Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp80-00810a004200010001-3: M. S. OKSYWIE","week":2,"year":1954},{"btih":"fd37410153fb766a169e778b750c56456cf03157","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1959-01-16T00:00:00Z","description":"Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 yWra L/Ct~L7aa:i6W.Q.vast v,yl~+aat;eb ` .. EBo .te, y v DEPARTMENT OF STATE Memorandum of Conversation L& IT DISTRIBUTION DATE: January 16,g 1959 10830 A.M. 12$45 P.M. SUBJECT: U-Soviet Relations Anastas I. Mikoyan9 Deputy Premier of the ESSR; Mikhail A. Menshikov, Soviet Ambassador,, Oleg A. Troyanovski9 Ministry of Foreign Affairs, USSR;; Aleksa dr Alekseevich Soldatov$ Ministry of Foreign Affairs,, USSR; Doha Foster ilulles, Secretary of State; Christian A. Herter, Under Secretary of State; Livingston T. Merchant, Assistant Secretary- Llewellyn E. Thompson9 American Ambassador to Moscow; Edward L. Freers, Director, Office of Eastern European Affairs S/S - 2 H Amembassy Moscow for Ambassador Thompson W INR Amembassy London for Ambassador Whitney G FE Amembassy Bonn for Ambassador Bruce C NEA Amembassy Rome for Ambassador Zeilerbach S/P L Amembassv Ottawa for Ambassador Wigglesworth Amembassy Paris for Ambassadors Houghton 8e Burgess The Secretary began by asking Mikoyan?a reaction to the reception he had received in the United States. The Secretary said that we believed in the right of peaceful demonstration but there were some people who did carry on activities which might seem offensive to guests. He hoped that Mikoyan has recognized that the American people are friendly as well as curious. Mikoyan said that he had had a chance to know something about the American people 22 years ago. He had received a good impression this time in spite of the activities of some immigrants. He felt the Russians and Americans could live together in peace and friendship. Twenty-two years ago he had traveled without a bodyguard, this time there was a great deal of security precaution. It would have been better without this, but apparently this had been impossible. The Secretary remarked that it showed how important he was now. Mikoyan rejoined by saying that it showed the change in times. Mikoyan remarked that he had gotten an better with the press than he had expected - either they had become better or he had been able to talk better with them., Businessmen had also been interested in his proposals. 22 years ago he had seen only a few officials of General Motors and Ford. This time he had seen many influential business leaders who had shown great interest. He had met Henry Ford the last time. This time he had met Henry Ford III,, who was also a pleasant man. He had met David Rockefeller9 who had expressed his regrets that the Moscow press and some people there seemed to think his family was war mongering and ST wanted INFORMATION COPY Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 SECRET a 2 wanted a deterioration in Soviet-American relations. Rockefeller had told Mikoyan that his family wanted an improvement in these relations no less than any other. The Secretary said he, himself, was tarnished with the charge of being a leading warMcnger, 2iikoyan remarked that he would not use the word \"tarnished\". The Soviets considered the Secretary as the leading strategist of the cold war, The Secretary suggested that when Mikoyan returned to Moscow,, he might review the Soviet propaganda line and might well find several respects in which it could be improved. Mikoyan said he would do this on the basis of reciprocity. The Secretary made the point that in the conversation with Mikoyan he might have to dwell on unpleasant topics. It was important to have a full and frank exchange of views. His attitudes were not personal ones but were basically shared by the people of the United States. Under our fora of society the individualistic viewpoint was not the governing one and individuals did not hold public office forever. This might give some satisfaction to the Minister but it would be short- lived because our policies would continue to go one Mikoyan said he understood the Secretary was referring to our Constitutional provision for a four-year incumbency by the ezocutive, The- Secretary said if a different party came to power Mikoyan could be sure its viewpoint would be the same. The Secretary said he did not think it would change as long as present conditions prevailed, He recalled that at the time of the Berlin blockade and airlift, when Governor Dewey had just been nominated and was expected to in the forthcoming electicnsa the first action taken by the Governor was to associate Senator Vandenberg and Secretary Dulles with him in a statement of complete support for the Democratic administration's position on Berlin. The Minister would be under a great illusion if he felt that the present attitude concerning Berlin would change with a change in administration. Mikoyan declared that the Soviets openly expressed the view that they saw no difference between the Democratic and Republiew _ ;% r, bat he thought the position required a change from a rigid to a more flexible attitude. The Secretary pointed out'that he had been under constant criticism from our Allies about inflexibility. Mikoyan said he had noted this and had noted that this criticism had had some influence an the Secretary. The Secretary said that.. this related perhaps to the formula for the reunification' of Germany through free elections, approved by the Summit Conference in July 1955. He wanted to say that we, did not alter an agreed policy unilaterally. Mikoyan asked whether we did not, however, change policies by agreement. The Secretary made the point that no policy was permanently unchangeable. We did not alter an agreed policy unilaterally but were always capable of changing it if it were reasonable to do so, He wanted to make clear that no formula about Berlin would be acceptable if the Soviets had in mind attempting, by working through the East German regime to impose its viewpoint. This could only lead to serious consequences, Mikoyan Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 ' Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 SECRET - 3 Mikayan said that what the Soviets had suggested was a six-month period for negotiating the problem. He wanted to make it very clear that the Soviet Union wanted to obtain no privileges in the situation -m either for itself or the German Democratic Republic - nor did it want to deprive the United States of anything. It wanted West Berlin to be neutralized and demilitarized under guarantees by the Great Powers or,, if necessary., by the UN. If we were agree- able, a permanent commission could be established to guarantee noninterference and freedom of access from all the sides. It welcomed other proposals that were based on noninterference. If nothing happened in six months,, the Soviets would turn over their powers to the GDR _- a state that has existed for seven years and on whose territory the lines of cannication lie. The Soviet Union did not seek changes in the internal system of West Berlin, It had no illusions that it-could be changed. The population could live under capitalism if it desired. Berlin (sic) could become a testing ground for cooperation between us Instead of a hot-bed of aggression. It could be a meeting spot for countries to come together. The Secretary stressed that we were prepared to negotiate but not only about Berlin and about a peace treaty,, but about all the questions involved. By other questions he meant steps to bring about reunification. The Soviet peace treaty draft was a call to perpetuate the division of Germany. The German problem should be discussed without any diktat as to subjects. Interrelated with all this -- Berlin,, peace treaty and East Germany - was the problem of European security. This close link was recognized at the Geneva Summit meeting and the problem of European security should be discussed too. The Secretary said the United States understood the Soviet preoccupations about Germany. If we could agree on reunification or a peace treaty, necessarily considered in the context of Europe as a whole,, the Soviet Union could and should be given assurances against the rebirth of German militarism. The United States wanted no political or stratetic advantage from the situation. The Secretary said the German problem was becoming more difficult to solve and any repetition of the dune 1953 outbreak which might take place would be occurring in much more dangerous circumstances. We recognized there were good and bad Germans and that the situation that evolved after World War I might evolve again. Mikoyan said he saw no reflection of our sympathetic attitude toward the Soviet Union in our proposals. In fact,, the Soviet leaders interpreted them in just the opposite way. They believed that lack of a peace treaty contributed to the perpetual division of Germany, Their draft did not assure reunification by itself but would bring conditions which would help rather than hinder reunification. Soviet objection to discussing reunification was not based on a position of diktat. Reunification was something to be brought about in the first instance by the two Germanies. They could be assisted but not replaced. The Federal Republic has refused to talk and has been supported in this by the r United States, To put Adenaueros position crudely, he wanted to try to annex the GDR; liquidate its socialist regime; remain in NATO; continue atomic ream 4 went; and bring about conditions which would allow German revanchists to become active when they would be strong enough to do so. The GDR would not want to SECRET liquidate INFORMATION COPY Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80M01009A000801140011-4 SECRET - 4 liquidate the socialist regime, but would resist. And, it was a Soviet ally in the Warsaw Pact,. The Soviets believed that the aim in raa.sing the problem as we raised it was to set our countries at loggerheads. Mikoyan said he had had many talks with Adenauer who showed no haste for reunification since he realized his approach to it was unrea and he could not accept any other approach. He preferred to wait until West Germany became stronger'and when with the help of the United States9 he could speak in a fifer ent tone, Mikoyan asked whether the United States position was that it did, in fact, want German reunification through annexation of the GDR to West Germany and did want the latter to remain armed and in NATO while the GDR withdrew from the Warsaw Pact and in that event was ready to reassure the Soviets by a type of pact which it now rejected. the Secretary said that he had tried to indicate in his press conference our complete philosophy about dealing with the German problem. We had to take into account the special position of countries lying next door to Germany and could not press any solution. But he wanted Mikoyan to have a chance to read carefully what the President and he had said* While he did not expect the Soviet Union to accept this to any extent now. he would welcome the chance to expound fully on it. He repeated that any reunification of Germany should be accompanied by security arrangements, treaties, or the like, which would bring us together, along with Germany** He did not think West Germany objected to this and he referred to the treaty we had proposed in 1946.. Mikoyan replied that we had had much in common in the 1945-116 period and our proposals had been more dramatic then than nowo He asked whether the Secretary had' anything mere specific or positive to say than what had been said at the press conference. The direction of the latter had been interesting bat he did not know where it led, He did not like the President's statement that we believed in Adenauer and that what had taken place in 1914 could not be the basis of our present policy. . .. Mikoyan said, reverting to an earlier topic, that the events of June 1953 could not oaglpq agsft because the West would not attempt to repeat them but because East Germany was now different. Errors in administration had been made but great changes had taken place in the past five years. Methods of administra- tion and government had improved, There was one socialist party but other parties as well. Unofficially, he could say that they had had to take repara- tions from East Germany because they did not get them from West Germany. Thus the economic situation had been'bad in 3953. The main thing was that the economic situation had improved. This was due to the fact that the Soviets had stopped taking reparations in 1953 and were now paying for all goods from East Germany and covering all occupation expenses, Aderiauer had taken a more aggressive'' tone by holding the Bindesstag meeting in West Birlin because he saw the situation improving in East Germany and felt he might lose any possibility of influencing it. The SED decided-at last year's party congress to match West German living standards y.1963, The socialist. regime was strong in East Germany. The Secretary SECRET INFORMATION COPY Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80M01009A000801140011-4 Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20 :CIA-RDP80M01009A000801140011-4 SECRET m 5 The Secretary referred to Mikoyancs remarks about alleged Western attempts to incite turmoil in East Germany, He said that.Mikoyan was quite wrong about this. The United States not only did not desire such troubles it would not like to see it happens This would be dangerous for all of use Mikoyan said the danger lay in our position and that there might be elements in West Germany takiig a different view from ours. These could bring about a deterioration in US-Soviet relations in spite of ourselves. Mikoyan said that free elections were no cure-all. The Soviet Union was not against them in principle but they were not the means of uniting Germany at present, o German States could not be eliminated by vote an interim stage was necessary. The Secretary said he wanted to talk about two other zones in which danger of war could arise. One was the Far East. Mere the Chinese Cc murai.sts were supported by the Soviet Union in the objective that the US must be*expelled by force from Taiwan and the West Pacific, Such a policy could have very serious consequences, Me United States would not be expelled by force or pressure from its collective security associations in the Western ' Pacific and Southeast Asia, China, like Korea, Vietnam and Germany, was divided. The US was friendly with one pare the Soviet Union with the other. Unification sought by force would almost ' surely lead to general war' We had exerted - great influence for restraint on President fhee who wanted to unify Korea by force. On our parts we could not be expelled by force where we were present by invitation or in fulfillment of formal agreements, Mikoyan said there' was no analogy in the situations mentioned - historically, juridically or in substance. In'Germany and in Korea zones of occupation bad been set up by victorious'allies, In Korea troops had been withdrawn at differ- ent dates, then war had Occurred -.there was no analogy with Germany' As to Mr' Rhee, the Soviets were not sure our professed restraint would always hold. North' Korea was note 'one big reconstruction site and might be envied by South Korea, The furrier had no intention to fight but if South Korea started, it would fight welll p as it had shown' 'In general it was a good idea to withdraw troops. There was a need for exchanges . between the Koreans' in the fields of culture and trade as a gradual means of bringing about roeunification, The division in Vietnam, according to Mikoyan, was the result of agreement reached at Geneva by all concerned, Turning to China, Mikayan said that the United States had been party to agreements that Taiwan should be returned to China along with the other islands. At me time it had not interfered in Chinese affairs mm a reasonable policy, useful for the United States. China would win in any case and this would be worse for the United States. After the remnants of counter-revolution had settled on Taiwan the United States had entered into a bilateral agreement and regarded Chiang Kai-shek as representing China. Treaties with him had not been accepted by the real Chinas No state would accept such unilateral actions. The Soviets were surprised by Chinese patience' Neither China nor the Soviet Union had ever sought to have the United States leave all the islands in the West SECRET INFORMATION COPY Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80M01009A000801140011-4 Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80M01009A000801140011-4 SECRET m 6 and troops there, Pacific. The United States had a treaty with the Philippine ~?~ it had allies in Singaporeq it had bases on Okinawa, They did not like this but were not attack- ing it, In general the Soviet Union wanted all foreign troops withdrawn and peaceful settlements guaranteed by the United Nations, If the United States left Okinawa, it would not be leaving the West Pacific, Since the United States did not want to ' leave under pressure of forces it should use the respite to leave voluntarily. It would not lose, but gain morals, political and military prestige if it. broke with Chiang Kai-shek and recognized the CPR,, The latter did not menace the United States., nor did the Soviet Unions The American position gave rise to more anti-Western feeling and tension in the area. The Secretary said Mikcyan had referred to the violation of the armistice in Korea as breaking up the possibility of reunification. This is what had happened at Taiwan. Mikoyan replied that he had been misunderstood, The Soviets did want re- unification of Korean He had made his remarks as information only and had had no specific purpose in making them, The Secretary said the Near Eastern situation was complex and he doubted whether he and Mikoyan could agree on airy of the elements in the situation, The area was vital to Western Europe as a source of oii, and as a means of communication between Asia and the Weser The United States had not believed that the military action by the ' UK9 France and Israel in 1956 had been the right way to protect their interestso This attitude should note howeverp be interpreted by the Soviet Union as reflecting any United States indifference to what took place there. ... . We were concerned about apparent efforts of International Cannn nism to gain control of the areas particularly about its activities in Iraq. Although the Soviets had been suspicious of American and British motives in responding to the appeals of Lebanon and Jordan, our withdrawal of troops had proven that we had had 'no intention of working to sustain Western influence in Iraq from outside. Mikoyan said the Soviets believedg, on the contraryn that that had been indeed our objective but that we had not been able to' bring it about Do public opinion had prevented use When the Secretary objected, Mikoyan said that both sides . would undoubtedly retain their own ideas about this. The Secretary said he was sorry about the Soviet view .d it had been disproved by our words and deeds. As soon as A UN 'fora had been founds we had withdrawn our troops. He said that,, on the other hands he hoped we could feel reasonably confident the Soviet Union did not desire to extend its control in Iraq and other Arab states. Mikoyan said the Soviets recognized the importance of the Middle East to the West 'as the source of Arabian oil and as the means of communication to Asia. iil:ganin and Khrushchev had made this point directly. The Soviets had, on several occasions, advanced proposals for a ffig Power meeting to work out common steps to prevent a further deterioration of the situation and to eliminate outside interference in the area. They had also made proposals about arms ship- ments. The Secretary Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80M01009A000801140011-4 Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 SECRET e 7 The Secretary said we had no quel with general principles but the area suggested in the Soviet proposals appeared too broad - stretching from Pakistan to Morocco. Mikoyan said the Soviets had been more interested in the Arab world and in Iran and Turkey in this connection. The Secretary said he had asked Gronyko in October 1957 for clarification of Soviet thinking about the scope of the area covered by their proposals but had not gotten it from him. Mikoyan said. they had been talking about the Near and Middle East -o certainly not Morocco -- the Near East was the main hotbed of tension here. They had acted on the assumption that the three Western Powers wanted to act in the area just as they pleased,., without asking the Arabs and without accepting the presence or interests of the Soviet Unions Mikoyan said the Secretary was wrong in suggesting there had been Soviet interference in Iraq. The Baghdad nations all had active intelligence services. They knew there had been no Soviet citizens involved. The Soviet leaders had not foreseen the revolution nor had they even heard of Kassem. The Secretary said he could be persuaded that the'Soviet Union had played no active part in the overthrow of the Nuri Government4, but he was talking about activi- ties that had taken place since then.. Mikoyan observed that if they had not interfered before the revolution it was strange to suggest that they were inter- fering now. They were glad that the revolution had occurred because itunder mi.n:ed the Baghdad Pact. a t, it was not the Communist Party alone but , other forces in Iraq as well who were supporting the legal government of Kass'em,, On the other hand, in the UAR, Nasser was arresting Communists. The S'oy dt Union had good relations with both countries. Its policy of noninterference was paying off for it in the Middle East. The Soviets had assured the Shah,-Of Iran that they would not interfere in Iranian affairs, although they didn't like his regime. 'He had given them assurances that Iran would not engage in any'inai itary arrangements directed against the Soviet Union nor allow foreign bases tc`be set up on Iranian soil, However., since the split in the Baghdad Pact there tad been certain developments and his policy seemed to have changed, Iran was providing military bases for the United States. We were thus interfering in the area,, not they. As to Pakistan, Mikoyan said he didn't know whether there were Comnniniste there or not. He had had'good relations with Mirza and had represented the USSR at the Constitution ceremonies, The Soviet attitude towards Ayub Khan'. was the same as toward the previous government, The Soviets saw no constitu- tional basis for his government, but this was a matter for the Pakistan people. Western policy in the Middle East was mistaken because it did not recognize that the colonial era had come. to an end. The Secretary said there had been much loose talk about the United States putting in new bases under new treaties with Tarkey8 Iran and Pakistan. This was not the case. The United States was engaged in fulfilling commitments already made. It had aaMutual. Act which laid out the terms and condi- tions for military assistance.31teas been going on has been talks about fulfilling its commitments to Turkey., Pakistan and Iran.-. These talks were designed to determine the measures needed to bring these commitments up to date. Mikoyan SECRET INFORMATION COPY Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4 S E C R E T B Mikoyan said .t ould be better to bury them rather than bring them UP to. dated The Secretary said that if Mikoyan saw the testa of the agreements the elves he 'would be reassured. Our recent 6ommitmeuts might result in some improvement in the military capability of Iran but in general all three countries In our view had excessive military estab- lishments in relation to their resources and:we favored greater dedication of the latter to esonomid'developmento oyan said that. the Soviet view was that the United States _ . to blame for these large military establish- ments and that we wanted to keep tension high the area through this polio SECRET INFORMATION COPY Declassified and Approved For Release 2013/05/20: CIA-RDP80MO1009A000801140011-4","downloads":28,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp80m01009a000801140011-4","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":12438332,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":3,"oai_updatedate":["2026-04-08T12:43:38Z","2026-04-08T14:42:56Z"],"publicdate":"2026-04-08T12:43:38Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp80m01009a000801140011-4: US-SOVIET RELATIONS","week":2,"year":1959},{"btih":"f6d83afa52afbea0fd7e49ee72a023a0f2e67438","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"2013-05-28T00:00:00Z","description":"Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/05/28: CIA-RDP82-00046R000100090008-3 ENCLO.SURE C Memory Sketch of RewRussian Single Line Landing and Take-off System. This system in use October 1952, jicorporates the use of only one runway for both landing and take-offs. SECRET 4 50X1 ororsoomm.srmil 1 pr I I I 11 I I 4), So mom ORM op. 4?. ijt SECRET Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/05/28: CIA-RDP82-00046R000100090008-3 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/05/28: CIA-RDP82-00046R000100090008-3 ENCLOSURE C (Contsd) SECRET - 2 - LEGEND 50X1 Point #1 through 7. same as corresponding points, Enclosure 27. 8, Red Flags: ijh polis, off strip. 9 through 19. 30 x 20 cm.; mounted on one-meter-showing width of landing and take-Same as corresponding points, Enclosure g. SECRET Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/05/28: CIA-RDP82-00046R000100090008-3","downloads":14,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00046r000100090008-3","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":842880,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":7,"oai_updatedate":["2026-06-17T04:48:29Z","2026-06-17T06:45:52Z"],"publicdate":"2026-06-17T04:48:29Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp82-00046r000100090008-3: (UNTITLED)","week":1,"year":2013},{"btih":"f6d9a9a97be1849f6b3314e68e9107d563a62467","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1953-04-01T00:00:00Z","description":"Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 PLACE NO. OF ENCLS. ACQUIRED (LIB BELOW) DATE SUPPLEMENT TO ACQUIRED REPORT NO. .. 25X1 M THIS IS UNEVALUATED INFORMATION 1. The ammunition dump for the 3rd Tank Div. was located in a wooded area three kilometers east of Jirice, six kilometers northeast of Benatecka Vrutice and seven kilometers northwest of'Milovice ?50-14,E14-57.The entire area was one kilometer long and 150 m. wide and was inclosed by two barbed wire fences two tothree meters high and three meters apart. The area between the-two fences was mined.'- 20' This ammunition dump was accessible by a double-lane, dirt road six meters wide, which branched off the Milovice-Jirice road from the south and the double-lane, cobblestone, six-meter-wide ,D,ipnik- Jirioe'road from the north. 25X1 4. No one, except the guards and the guard commander, was located in the area, which was restricted to all other personnel, except the Division Ordnance Officer and the Division officer of the Day. S hould i be necessary for any one to edter the area, he had to be accompanied by the Division Ordnance Officer and the Guard. c ommander. in all, there were 20 guards, one guard NCO and one guard commander on duty in the area. The guard detail changed, every 24 bra. at 1700 bra. This detail consisted of personnel from the various units within the First and Second Camp Areas of.the 3rd Tank Div. All the guards were armed with submachine guns and were posted at various points in the building area and in the guard towers. The guard was doubl.ed.at night. Field t elephones were located 4n4he--b'uf- sings designated as points. USAF review completed. Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 SECRET #5, #6, #11, #15, #18 and #22 in- sketch of the area--7 25X1 The save type of password system ryas n effect 25X1 as in rd Camp Areas 25X1 Enclosure,A Sketob of the Anwimlition Dump, 3rd Tank Div. SECRET Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 SECRET -3- 'Anclosure A sketch of the Ammunition.-Dump, 3rd Tank Div. SECRET Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7 Enclosure A (CONT'D) SECRET -4- All.dimensions are approximate. POINT # 1. Entrance: the only entrance to the saw' itioa dump; oms s ed of-a steel mesh gate two meters wide and two meters high; was always locked. #;2,3' s7,,8,10,13, and 14. Under ound Ammunition Bunkers: dirt-covered undo. each m. long T m. wide, an 10 an. hi 25X1 The entrance* o ese ounKi ns{ stmA of concrete steps two meters be? nr \"nnnd co # 5,6,9,11,12, and 18. Ammunition Storage Huts: above-ground, one-story, gray, stuccoed-brick -ui ings, each 15 x be roofs. # 15.and 22. Guard Towers.: brown wooden structures 21 X 21 X 2 a.,_ :w a ar-papered gable roofs; built on wooden supports four to six meters high; each was manned by one guard armed with a subP%achine gun during the hours of daylight; an additional guard was posted during. the hours of darkness ; also mounted in each tower was a 7.92 mm. light, machine gun; there was also an electric alarm system hooked up from each tower to the guard house. # 16. Mine Field: three meters wide; between the two fences fonts 0 and #217; the mines were laid at an unknown depth and at intervals; the mined area was also strung with.barbed wire. Facing the inside of the area and posted on trees, near the inside fence at various inter- vals were wooden : igns on which. the warning Attention Mines (Pdzor mint) was painted iri red. # 17. monition Dump-: a wooded area, one kilometer long and 156 m. wide. # 19'. Guard House: aone-story, brown, wooden building reateit'h a brown preservative; 8 X 6 X 4 m.; tar- papered, gable roof; one guard commander and 20 military guards were housed here. # 20. Outside Fence: steel wire mesh; two meters high, with 1 1. three strands of barbed wire strung on top. # 21. Inside Fence: a four-strand, barbed-wire fence; one meter g and three meters from the outside fence. # 23. Road: a double-lane dirt road; six meters wide; serviced TH-eammunition dump and was connected with the Milovice-_ Jirice road and the Lipnik-Jirice road. # 24. Road: a single-lane dirt road; branched off the road a rove and led directly to the ammunition dump. This road was closed to civilian traffic. Approved For Release 2009/08/07: CIA-RDP82-00046R000200060015-7","downloads":36,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00046r000200060015-7","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":3305175,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":2,"oai_updatedate":["2025-04-21T07:43:05Z","2026-03-27T08:34:20Z"],"publicdate":"2025-04-21T07:43:05Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp82-00046r000200060015-7: AMMUNITION DUMP OF THE 3RD TANK DIVISION, MILOVICE AREA","week":1,"year":1953},{"btih":"39b086a97a43121e1502fe3a469f91ef4c348081","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1953-08-31T00:00:00Z","description":"Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/06/12 : CIA-RDP82-00046R000100200008-0 50X1 50X1 COUNTRY Poland SECORJTY iNFORMATiON SUBJECT 1. The YAK-9p Fighter 2. Landing Gear of the MIG -15 PLACE ACQUIRED DATE ACQUIRED DATE OF IN THIS IS UNEVALUATED INFORMATION REPORT NO. 50X1 DATE DISTR. 31 Aug. 53 NO OF PAGES 5 NO OFENCLS. 1 (LISTED SELOWI SUPPLEMENT TO REPORT NO. SOURCE 50X1 1. The pre-flight check of the YAK-9p fighter was carried out as follows: Prior to starting the engines, the pilot circled the aircraft, giving it a visual check. He checked the fuel tanks, gear, ailerons, and elevator, and made sure that the pitot cover and locks were removed. The cowling was removed from the engine and, as the mechanic operated various controls in the cockpit, the pilot looked for leaks from the lines to the engines. The cowl-ing was then replaced and the pilot entered the aircraft. This check was SOP and was performed each morning as well as every time the aircraft was refueled. Because of very poor maintenance at the Pilots' Training School, the above check was also made before each flight. 2. The starting procedure was as follows: The battery was switched on, the primer pumped, the propeller was pitched forward, the throttle was cracked, the engine compression air starter valve was opened, the booster coil energizing button was depressed, the magneto switch was turned to 1 plus 2 position, the throttle was worked until the engine started and the engine compression air starter valve was closed. ? SECURITY INFORMATION Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/06/12 : CIA-RDP82-00046R000100200008-0 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/06/12 : CIA-RDP82-00046R000100200008-0 SECRET 50X1 3. The Ohly-Check.mate just prior to the take-Off was.. that of the Magneto at 2100 rpm;) \"Althiough-t-50 rpm Magneto drOp was the makiMUM, alloWable$ the UsUal drop on each magneto was 150 rpm. -Hi' since all the aircraft were in the same?condition'and the abOveAtas true for all aircraft, the pilot* did not heed the rpm drop but flew the aircraft anyway Otherwise they would not ?'hive-Obtained-their-necessary flying time. During ? the 'magneto Cheek, the tail section had to be tied down or held down by throe 160Aanice.. 4. The YAK-9p needed the following distances for take-offs and landings: ?Take-off: 380 m. Landing, using brakes: 550 m. Landing, not using brakes: 1,200 m. 5. The YAK-9p performed as follows at different power settings: rpm's 141?._! Altitude _ ;peed Range Radius Time 1,600 unk. 1,000 m. 300 km./hour 635 I. unk. 3,000 unk. 1,000 m. 555 km./hour 335 km. 1 hour plus 30 60 The landing gear system of the MIG-15 was operated by an eleotro-meohanical aotuator. I have no furbher information. * M.P. - Manifold Preisure ENCLOSURE: A.. Sketch, with Legends of the Cockpit of the YAK-9p Fighter. SECRET Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/06/12 : CIA-RDP82-00046R000100200008-0 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/06/12 : CIA-RDP82-00046R000100200008