 48. operating Valve for Hydraulic Landimg Gear. 47. Oxygen Pressure Gauge 48. Oxygen Flow Indicator SECRET neclassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/06/12 : CIA-RDP82-00046R000100200008-0","downloads":54,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00046r000100200008-0","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":4248768,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":23,"oai_updatedate":["2026-06-17T07:07:34Z","2026-06-17T08:42:07Z"],"publicdate":"2026-06-17T07:07:34Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp82-00046r000100200008-0: 1. THE YAK-9P FIGHTER 2. LANDING GEAR OF THE MIG-15","week":14,"year":1953},{"btih":"97a14fb153d77689175ce68b0fa37c8e4a15762f","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1954-03-24T00:00:00Z","description":"Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved - for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: 114,17 CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 REPORT NO. 112111MINSISOWININFINIS, 50X1 50X1 COUNTRY USSR DATE DISTR. 24 March 1964 SUB3ECT Soviet Interior Troops NO. OF PAGES 4 DATE OF INFORMATION REFERENCES: 50X1 PLACE ACQUIRED SOURCE 50X1 THIS IS UNEVALUATED INFORMATION Operational units, Convoy Troops, and Railway Troops were not subordinate to the Main Administration of Interior Troops (GUIVV - Glavnoye Upravleniye Vnutrennykh Yoysk), Mosoow. He gave the following background information to clarify the status of the above mentioned troops. a. After the 1917 revolution, police and security functions were exercised first by the All Union Extraordinary Commission (VCHK - Vsesoyuznaya Chrezvychaynaya Komissiya - called CHEKA), and from 1922 or 1923 by the State Political Administration Agency (OGPU - Organ Gosudarstvennogo Politicheskogo Upravleniya). In the thirties, possibly in 1935, the OGPU was replaced by the People's Commissariat of Internal Affairs (NKVD - Narodnyy Komissariat Vnutrennykh Del). About 1940, an additional agency, the People's Commissariat of State Security (NKOB - Narodnyy Kommissariat Gosadarstvennoy Bezopastnosti) was established. About 1946, the letters \"NK\" in NKVD and NKGH were changed to \"M\" (Ministerstvo - Ministry) and the MVD and MOB came into existence. _SECRET. Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 SECRET - 2 - The NKVD/MVD had at its organization the following troops, all on the same level with each other and subordinated to the NKVD/ MVD: Interior (Vnutrennyye), Railway,(Zheleznodorozhnyye), Convoy (KonVoynyye), Border Buard (POgranichnyye), Guard Troops for Special Objects (Po Okhrany Osobykh Ob'yektov), and Militia, the city and county police. 50X1 d. Until 1944, the and officials or staffs). In Troops (Operational ment (Voyska Osol:ogo In 1950 or 1952, Troops, and Nr-itia MVD was left iith Troops for Special NKGB/MGB had no uniformed troops, only offices (called \"organs\", a Soviet term for headquarters 1944, the NKOB activated Operational (Operativnyye) units), also called Troops for Special Commit-Naznacheniya). control of the Border Guard Troops Interior was transferred from the MVD to the MOB. The the Railway Troops, Convoy Troops, and Guard Objects. 50X1 e. the MOB was completely deactivated. Some 1:101.t and offices still Used the MOB seal and stamp, but all Boyder Troops., Interior subordinate to Troops, Operational Troops, and Militia became the MVD-. 50X1 the MVD had the following subordinate Main Adminis-50X1 50X1 trations, all on an equal level: Interior Troops, Convoy Troops, Railway Troops, Operational Troops, Border Guard Troops, Guard Troops for Special Objects, and Militia. Strength and T/0 known to Source are given in para. 3, below. Local fire departmpts were also included in uniformed MVD personnel. 2. Locations of Border Guard Troops units known to Source were given in He also knew 91 tne ena ana 4tn mvi) viva., prooaoly bpecial uommitment (O$NAZ - Osobogo Naznacheni a), located in Vilna 5 54-20, E 25-207 and l Shauli (Siaulai) 55-58, E 22-3_327. He did not know which division 'IfiAs at Which location. He knew that the let Sep. Moscow Rifle Div. of Special Commitment (Pervaya Osobaya Moskovskaya Strelkovaya Diviziya Osobogo.Naznacheniya) was stationed in Moscow in 1952.- He could not give any additional locations or numerical identifications. He stated that the units were scattered all over the USSR and probably in Soviet-occupied territory. .Source had no data on headquarters units of Operational Troops, Convoy Troops, Railway Troops, Guard Troops for Special Objects, Interior Troops, or Militia. He was of the opinion that these units had the same headquarters organization as Border Guard Troops (See . He gave the following Tb b information on subordinate units of each type of MVD troops: Operational Troops - Source believect:that a division had 8,000 - 12,000 officers and EM, and consisted of three regiments plus service, supply, signal, engineer, and transportation units. Each regiment had about 2,000 officers and EM, and consisted of three battalions, plus service, supply, signal, engineer-, and trans-portation units. Each battalion had about 500 officers and EM, And Consisted of three companies. Each company had about 125 officers and EM, and consisted of three platoons of three squads each. A squad had 1 NCO and 12 EM. He knew no further T/0 of Operational Troops. Interior Troops - Source believed that the highest unit was a regiment, which was identical to a regiment of Operational Troops (see above). He knew no further T/0 of Interior Troops. SECRET Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 50X1 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 SECRET -3-50X1 itarder_thiarl Troops - For T/0, Militia - T/0 unknown Convoy Troops - T/0 unknown Railway Troops - TA unknown ? Guard Troops for Special Objects - T/10 unknown b. Source gave the following strength breakdown of MVD troops: Operational Troops - About 100,000. Source knew of three divi-sions (see para. 2, above). He did not know the total number of divisions. 50X1 50X1 50X1 50X1 Interior Troops - About 150,000 Border Guard Troops - 300,000. The Border Guard Troops had IIC detachments, each with about 2,500 officers and EM. From various orders, and hearsay at the Senior MOB Officers' School, Source heard of all detachments from 1 to 110, and was sure that there were 110. Including OCS students and border district (Okrug) personnel, Source estimated total strength at no less than 300,000 officers and EM. Militia - Unknown. In a conversation in 1947 with (194)4), and later Militia, Moscow, Source were 130,000 armed and uniformed Militia personnel in Moscow alone. Convoy Troops - About 100,000 - 150,000 Railway Troops - About 100,000 Guard Troops for Special Objects About 150,000 - 200,000 SMAx:de,heard that the overall strength of the MVD/MGB troops was about 1,000,000, not including the Militia. All figures above were based on hearsay and conversations with other officers. c. Source believed that all units were equipped with only small arms, such as pistols, carbines, and MO's. He never heard of any units having tankts, artillery, or mortars. Source believed that all units had an unknown number of trucks, sedans, grenades, transceivers, switchboards, telephone equipment, engineer tools, etc. No further details on weapons and equipment. d. He was positive that Convo Troops were independent units directly subordinate to the MVD During 1941-1943, Convoy Troops were attached to Interior Troops for speoial missions. Political Officer, learned that there Main Administration of 50X1 ? Source gave the following information on the guarding of important installations by MVD troops: Railway Troops - Guarded trains, depots, tunnels, railroad bridges, and railroad stations. Guard Troops for Special Objects - Guarded forced labor camps, prisons, armament factories, government buildings atomic plants, experimental factories, and laboratories. SECRET Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7 50X1 SECRET - 4 - 50X1 Interior Troops - Guarded air fields and Party buildings. Operational Troops - Normally had no such duties. Source saw units of the 1st Sep. Moscow Rifle Div. of Special Commitment guarding the parade grounds during the 7 November parade in Moscow. Convoy Troops - No such duties. Border Guard Troops - No such duties. Militia - No such duties. important persons were guarded by MGB officers. Source did not know the present system used, or if MVD troops had such duty. He have the following information on the use of MVD troops for suppression, collectivization, relocation, etc.: Railway Troops - Operated trains transporting groups going to forl labor camps, or groups being relocated. Guard Troops for Special Objects - The only duties of ibese troops connected with suppression, etc., were guarding forced labor camps and prisons. Interior Troops - Used for suppression of revolts. Operational Troops - Used for special duties such as riot control, suppressions of subversive elements, resettlement of dissident groups, and combat of revolts by ethnic groups. Convoy Troops - Escorted prisoners in transit and helped in deporta-tion operations... Border Guard Troops - Secured Soviet frontiers against entry of alien agents and saboteurs, and prevented the escape of illegal border crossers. Militia - Was responsible for the local control of the populace. Source stated that the Militia was the most brutal and most hated agency in the MVD. Control of the population in event of disaster was handled by the Militia, and other MVD troops usually had no such duties. However, in the 1945 earthquakes at Ashkhabad 21 37-57, E 58-217, Interior Troops were brought in to aid the Militia in the control of looters and to guard banks, railroads, and Party and government buildings. SECRET 50X1 Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release @ 50-Yr 2013/09/26: CIA-RDP82-00046R000300260012-7","downloads":19,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp82-00046r000300260012-7","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":5311848,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":9,"oai_updatedate":["2026-06-17T07:04:57Z","2026-06-17T08:59:24Z"],"publicdate":"2026-06-17T07:04:57Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp82-00046r000300260012-7: SOVIET INTERIOR TROOPS","week":2,"year":1954},{"btih":"a9cf3f85cef5b40a8877e54ea243873967c754fd","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1970-10-26T00:00:00Z","description":"Approved For Release 2007/01/31 : CIA-RDP73B00296R000100020052-2 OLC 70-0763 26 October 1970 ORANDUM FOR THE RE C T: Conversation with Bill Woodruff, C Senate Appropriations Committee data John Maury and I met with Bill Woodruff to discuss the possibility of the Director visiting Senator Russell in Winder, Georgia. The Director previously advised us that the desirability of such a meeting had come up in a conversation between Senator Russell and Secretary Laird and was relayed to the Director by Richard Capon, Assistant to the Secretary of Defense (Legislative Affairs). 2. Woodruff said Capen had been \"badgering\" him all wek- end about this and had talked with him again this morning, Woodruff plans to talk with Senator Russell about it this afternoon. Woodruff is o be involved in this kind of meeting and said he hoped it did not tarn out to be a situation where the two men got together on the assumption that the other had requested the meeting. He added that Senator Russell would be busy with personal family matters through Wednesday of this week and he doubted that anything would develop before Thursday or Friday, if at all. that the Director is willing to see Senator Russell anytime . the Senator desires and he obviously wants to talk with the Senator about his recent trip to Southeast Asia. We made it clear, however, that the Director was not pressing to set Senator Russell in Georgia and could wait until the Senator returns to Washington although he would like to have a private meeting with him prior to our budget session, tentatively scheduled for 19 November. Approved For Release 2007/01/31 : CIA-RDP73B00296R000100020052-2 Approved For Release 2007/01/31 : CIA-RDP73B00296R000100020052-2 SECRET 4. Woodruff was obviously irritated with both Laird and Capon over a number of things including this latest development which Woodruff believes was Inspired more by the Secretary than Senator Russell. d Capon has talked with him about Secretary Laird's desire that Senator Russell \"pull Senator Mender off the Defense Appropriations bill\" when it comes up for floor debate in the Senate. He believes that Laird is pushing the idea that if Russell doesn't manage the Defense bill that he have Senator Stennis manage it and Woodruff made it clear that he would have no part of this latter arrangement. He said DOD was having enough problems in the Executive Branch without trying to run the Congress. 5. We then talked about the budget meeting. Woodruff said we should be prepared to discuss the request for a supplement 25 1A to our Reserve even though it I. fairly clear that the Senate Subcommittee will approve onl (He indicated that this would be treated as al cut. Hs suggested we take the position that we might very well need the depending on circumstances. Woodruff recalls that Colonel White said at our regular budget hearing in May that we were very concerned about the level of our Reserve and Haight have to come back to our Committee to increase it. 6. Woodruff also suggested that we discuss the matter of the impact of the iibright amendment on programs in Southeast Asia. He recommends that we review these programs individually and discuss the impact of the amendment as we understand it. He said the Sub- committee would also want a world wrap-up at this time. 7. With regard to the Fuibright amendment, Woodruff again no question but that the amendment language will be in tense bill as it is reported out of the Senate Committee. He went on to say that if Senator Russell manages the bill he will merely call the Senator's attention to some of the problems Involved and let the Senator decide how to handle it. On the other hand, if Senator Ellender handles the bill, he will recommend that Eilender ask Laird and the Director to review our programs with Senator Fulbright before the bill goes to the floor. Distribution: Deputy Legislative ounse Orig. - Subj. (via DCI) I - Lx Di r I - OGC I - OPPB CLC /GLCsrw (27 Oct 70) /01 l RDP73B00296R000100020052-2","downloads":38,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp73b00296r000100020052-2","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":2212949,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":7,"oai_updatedate":["2024-11-19T13:30:08Z","2026-03-17T06:04:39Z"],"publicdate":"2024-11-19T13:30:08Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp73b00296r000100020052-2: CONVERSATION WITH BILL WOODRUFF, COUNSEL, SENATE APPROPRIATIONS COMMITTEE","week":1,"year":1970},{"btih":"ef0384b0763400e7f347829cd32660db3abaeeb9","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1971-09-03T00:00:00Z","description":"Approved For Re4pase 2002/06/17 78-06363AOGO300050024-1 ULVTTET 25X1A8a 25X1A8a MEMORANDUM FOR: Director of Training 3 September 1971 SUBJECT : Weekly Activities Report - Language School 1. I I and I met with of on Monday to discuss long-range Chinese require- ments. FE is presently in the middle of long-range analysis of their needs for placing people overseas. They promised to provide us some data on training requirements sometime in October. In broad terms they felt that their input of beginning language students would probably drop. However, they predicted that, since there is a large body of personnel who already have some Chinese language skills, they would probably have requirements for refresher or maintenance training. 2. The Linguistic Steering Committee has concerned itself in recent weeks with the standardization of language tapes produced at IS. As the first of its proposed guidelines, the committee recommended a unified tape labeling system. Since our tape collection has been put together by several different hands over the years, and since tape formats have changed with the evolution of new approaches to language teaching, our current labeling system is not consistent. The new system will not only introduce consistency, but also more clarity and convenience for the tape user. Box labels will be typed instead of hand stamped; labels will identify not only the series and tape number, but also give an indication of the type of material on the reel. A special large-type selectric typewriter ball is the new ingredient that makes the new labeling system practicable. We hope to make extensive use of the secretarial pool for help in typing up the new labels. 3. Danish is a language for which we have a stand-by instructor, but which we very rarely teach. This week Mrs. an 25,X1A9a independent contract teacher who has occasionally taught part-time tutorials at home, joined our faculty to teach a full-time Danish class at IS. The course is scheduled to last 28 weeks. Approved For Release 2002/06/17 . - P78-06363A000300050024-1 Approved For Release 2002/06/17 : CIA-RDP78-06363A000300050024-1 SECRET -__ 4. language School Statistics: Having reached their nadir in the last month, enrollment figures are now beginning to climb again. About 60 requests have been approved for classes beginning at LS on 13 September and 119 for the BAHLT program. Here are the figures for the week of 23 - 27 August 1971: Students Classes Full-time - 32 Full-time - 18 Part-time - 65 (28 Hqs.) Part-time - 29 (6 Hqs.) TOTA.L...... 97 TOTAL ...... 47 Laboratory hours for the week of 23 - 27 August 1971: language School - 49 There were 43 proficiency tests given during the week of 23 - 27 August 1971. 25X1A9a 5. our two summer employees, left us this week after a busy three months. Because of the great changes in the lab and library that were brought about by the move to new quarters, there was a lot to do in those two areas. We were very happy to have these two young people with us. They did an excellent job. Chief, Language School/TR SECRET Approved For Release 2002/06/17 : CIA-RDP78-06363A000300050024-1","downloads":61,"format":["Additional Text PDF","Archive BitTorrent","DjVuTXT","Djvu XML","Image Container PDF","Item Tile","Metadata","OCR Page Index","OCR Search Text","Page Numbers JSON","Scandata","Single Page Processed JP2 ZIP","chOCR","hOCR"],"identifier":"cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp78-06363a000300050024-1","indexflag":["index","nonoindex"],"item_size":1458817,"language":"english","mediatype":"texts","month":4,"oai_updatedate":["2023-08-30T10:22:48Z","2026-03-20T19:30:58Z"],"publicdate":"2023-08-30T10:22:48Z","subject":"CIA Reading Room","title":"CIA Reading Room cia-rdp78-06363a000300050024-1: WEEKLY ACTIVITIES REPORT - LANGUAGE SCHOOL","week":3,"year":1971},{"btih":"a8f090186b2660bda16e29500273ec451eb7efed","collection":["ciareadingroom","usgovernmentmirrors","government-documents"],"creator":"CIA Reading Room","date":"1953-10-09T00:00:00Z","description":"Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release 2014/03/04 : CIA-RDP81-01030R000100240007-7 oullow5 50X1 CENTRAL INTELLIGENCE AGENCY INFORMATION REPORT This Document contains information affecting the Na-Uonal Defense of the United States, within the mean-ing of Title 18, Sections 783 and 794.01 the U.S. Code. as amended. Its transmission or revelation of Its contents to or receipt by an unauthorized penion Is prohibited by law. The reproduction of this form Is prohibited. SECURITY INFORMATION 50X1 COUNTRY USSR (Moscow Oblast) REPORT SUBJECT German Scientists at Zavod 393, DATE DISTR. Krasnogorpk 9 October 1953 NO. OF PAGES 12 DATE OF INFO. REQUIREMENT 50X1 PLACE ACQUIRED REFERENCES 50X1-HUM THE .SOURCE EVALUATIONS IN THIS REPORT ARE DEFINITIVE. THE APPRAISAL OF CONTENT IS TENTATIVE. (FOR KEY SEE REVERSE) ?. 50i1-HUM? 1. Comments 2. Read Zagorsk for Sagorsk throughput the re-port. 50X1-HUM ' STATE #x ADM fix II A VY #x AIR Fe I AEC (Note: Washington Distribution Indicated By \"X\": Field Distributio By \"#\".) Declassified in Part - Sanitized Copy Approved for Release 2014/03/04: CIA-RDP81-61030R000100240007-7 50X1 ? - ??---_ Declassified in Part -Sanitized-Copy Approved for Release 2014/03/04: CIA-RDP81-01030R000100240007-7 SECRET SECURITY INFORMATION COUNTRY : USSR REPORT 50X1-HUM DATE DISTR. / 7 4 0 .53 SUBJECT : German Personnel at Krasnogorsk NO. OF PAGES 11 PLACE ACQUIRED ,DATE ACQUIRED DATE OF It THIS IS UNEVALUATED INFORMATION NO. OF ENCLS. (LISTED BELOW) SUPPLEMENT TQ 50X1-HUM REPORT NO. 50X1-HUM ? 1. The following members of